			<font face="Arial" size="-1" color="Black">
<!-- From -->
From Ubiq on the <a class=post href=http://boards.stratics.com/cgi-bin/forum/origin/postlist.pl?Cat=&Board=uo2devboard&page=1&view=expanded&sb=5>Stratics DevBoard</a>:
<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
You guys sure are reading a lot into d00dism.  I think the truth may be a lot simpler than this.<br><br>People who talk in d00dspeak do it because that is part of the culture that they are in.  Just as those who roleplay surround themselves with others who roleplay and they learn and feed off of that, those who talk and learn 1337sp34k do it by hanging out with other people they feel comfortable with.  These are often (but not always!) young kids, often those who spend lots of time in the internet, and who frequently have habits in their 'speech' and attitudes towards other people shaped by their time in IRC chat rooms.<br><br>They don't roleplay because (a) they feel it's unimportant, (b) they think it's kind of wierd, and (c) they think it's unnecessary.  It's easy for us to jump all over people like this, but think back to when you were young, playing paper games back in school.  I certainly didn't roleplay back in junior high - me and my friends did monty haul hackmaster type campaigns.  We sure thought we were roleplaying, but we weren't.<br><br>As for grief players (which I suspect what this article was about, deep down), I've found that there are two kinds of grief players.  The first is the kind that does not grok that there is another person on the other end of the telephone wire, and/or does not know what is acceptable behavior in this little community that we've created.  These people need to be socialized and educated as far as what the standards of the community are - or they will forever do things that tick you off.<br><br>The other kind of grief player is the kind that knows full well that there is someone on the other end of the telephone wire, and enjoys tormenting them in order to feel power over them.  Unfortunately, there is little good response to these people other than to ban them.  Fortunately, there are fewer bad people than you would suppose, but like many things, the bad ones tend to be memories bad enough to make it difficult to remember the pleasant encounters in a MMPRPG.<br><br>Ubiq
<!-- /Indent --></font></td></tr></table><!-- End /Indent -->
<!-- End Story -->
<!-- Thanks -->
Thanks!
<!-- End Thanks -->
</font>


