			<font face="Arial" size="-1" color="Black">
<!-- From -->
From Ubiq on the <a class=post href=http://boards.stratics.com/cgi-bin/forum/origin/postlist.pl?Cat=&Board=uo2devboard&page=0&view=expanded&sb=5>Stratics DevBoard</a>:
<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
We haven't commented on height and weight yet, because we're not sure we can get it in, quite simply.  The problem, perversely, is that of our clothing.  If you just paint all of the clothing on over a single mesh, doing height and weight deformation is a simple task.  However, once you do what we are doing (i.e. ripping apart your mesh and rebuilding it at run-time, so that clothes actually change the shape of your avatar), height and weight deformation becomes computationally expensive and technically challenging.<br><br>This is complicated by clothes that hang.  Check out this screenshot: <A class=post HREF="http://origin.stratics.com/sections/sod/screens/origin2.jpg" target="_new">http://origin.stratics.com/sections/sod/screens/origin2.jpg</A>.  The coat that he has on hangs on him.  When the character animates, it is very difficult to do things like keep the legs from passing through the coat when he runs or fights.  Allowing players to grow or shrink their avatar makes this even harder on the art staff.<br><br>We still have a couple of avenues that we can explore, and Melior hasn't given up on it yet, but it is a challenge, so I wouldn't count on it until you hear otherwise.<br><br>Ubiq
<!-- /Indent --></font></td></tr></table><!-- End /Indent -->
<!-- End Story -->
<!-- Thanks -->
Thanks!
<!-- End Thanks -->
</font>


