<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
<b>Crafters Gaining Skill</b><br>We're still working on getting the movie ready to show.  here's the thing though....it takes time, and that's a commodity the team just doesn't have right now.  With Thanksgiving coming up (for non-Americans, it's a holiday we celebrate every year by eating way too much food) I wouldn't expect it for a little while.<br><br>Just hold on.....that's all I can say.<br><br>As for crafter's gaining skill.....well, that's still something they're working on right now.  I think it would be premature to say anything about it at this time.<br><br><br>"G." Bob<br>Community Coordinator<br>"Nemo enim fere saltat sobrius, nisi forte insanit."<p><b>Misc Items For Hyperion</b><br><!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent--><i>1. Any estimate as to how many stars there are in the sky (all of it, both hemispheres)?<br><br>2. What is the name of the largest constellation of stars in the sky, and how many visible stars is it made up by? On a side-question... any favourite constellation?<br><br>3. Will there be a "northern star" that we, theoretically, can use to navigate the seas at night? Does it have a name?<br><br>4. Will we be able to use spyglasses to see celestial objects more clearly, and perhaps see things we otherwise would be unable to see?<br><br>5. I've read that the night makes up 1/3 of the day/night cycle, but nothing on the length of dusk and dawn... Will it be 1/3 day, 1/6 dusk, 1/3 night and 1/6 dawn or something like that? Also, how much can the day/night length vary depending on Sosaria's tilt and time of year?<br><br>6. Will any star have the ability to vary in apparent size and/or brightness?<br><br>7. Will stars blink and fickle due to "atmospheric turbulence" or shine in a constant way?<br><br>8. Are there clouds thick enough to entirely block the visibility of stars behind?<br><br>9. Will there be any other objects in the sky than the sun, the stars and the moons (such as left-over Overlord satellites, comets, meteors et.c.)?<br><br>10. If we used a spyglass to look at the sun (in "UWO:Origin" - I already know the answer for "Real Life"), would we suffer any kind of "negative" effect or would there be an invisible monkey there to absorb the unhealthy light before it damages our eyes?<br><br>11. Will there be rainbows?</i><!-- /Indent --></font></td></tr></table><!-- End /Indent -->1. Depends on hardware performance and LOD settings. I've cranked it up to 100,000 stars as a test. You probably won't be able to do this in the ship client. Most of those stars were barely visible because the LOD system adds stars at the lesser magnitude end. Think of it as adjusting the radius of a sphere in which you can see stars.<br><br>2&3. Not sure. We haven't done a final pass on the sky yet, so positions of the major stars will probably be moved.<br><br>4. I don't know if there will be spyglasses in the game. If there were, you would be able to see celestial objects more clearly.<br><br>5. The day/night length should be fairly realistic. I'm not sure how to measure dawn and dusk. Those are pretty fuzzy terms. There will be more daylight in the summer and less in the winter - gameplay takes place in the northern hemisphere.<br><br>6. Do you mean over time?<br><br>7. Stars twinkle in a subtle fashion.<br><br>8. Yes.<br><br>9. Not at this time.<br><br>10. This might depend on which way you held the spyglass.<br><br>11. Sorry, no rainbows.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>Concerning Difficulty of Creatures</b><p>This is actually a common problem in MMORPGs.<br><br>Developers create a creature that they assume could *never* be defeated by a single player (Ubiq, recently, brought up the example of the Yeti in Merdian 59).  As time goes on though, players find a way to solo the creature in question.  As the game goes on, creatures that once brought a feeling of fear amongst the players now become a simple kill.<br><br>We think Dragons are prety darn mean.  We know, however, that players are smart.  Really, really smart.  Too darn smart for their own good. :)<br><br><br><br>"G." Bob<br>Community Coordinator<br>"Nemo enim fere saltat sobrius, nisi forte insanit."<p><b>Playing In A Window</b><br>Gaffer recently had some thoughts on these lines, but the problem breaks down to two things.<br><br>Exploits: Let's face it, it's easier to make a cheat program for a game not played in a window than with one that is.  I think most people don't want to see the world filed with imbalancing cheat programs.<br><br>Performance: In a 3-D environment, playing inside a window can reallt hurt performance.  We're really pushing the envelope with the client, and it would be a shame to give an option that would make the client unplayable on some machines.<br><br>Now, those are two real concerns, but we still haven't made up our mind.  We understand how important it is for people to be able to do things like check e-mail or browse the web while playing.  We also understand that programs that assist in communication are very important to players.  What will we decide?  I don't know, but we're more than willing to listen to people's thoughts on this matter.<br><br><br>"G." Bob<br>Community Coordinator<br>"Nemo enim fere saltat sobrius, nisi forte insanit."<p><b>Item Decay</b><br>Items that are left on the ground in the game world that are not locked down will decay and eventually be removed from the world.<br><br>Medwynd<br>User Interface Programmer<br>UWO: Origin<p><b>Concerning The Camera</b><br>The camera position is movable so the game is not just viewable from behind your back.  You can move the camera in, out, and rotate it around your avatar.  A fixed view, i.e. Diablo or UO, is possible if you position your camera that way but you would really be missing out on a lot of the look and feel of the world.<br><br>Medwynd<br>User Interface Programmer<br>UWO: Origin<p><b>Clothing</b><br>Something we are very proud of here is the wide selection of clothing.  I think you'll be quite happy with the results. :)<br><br><br>"G." Bob<br>Community Coordinator<br>"Nemo enim fere saltat sobrius, nisi forte insanit."<p><b>% Chance To Hit</b><br>I was actually working from home Friday, so apparently my "Hiding" skill has gone up to the point where GBob couldn't find me to test :)<br><br>GBob's right though - it's tough to boil down to a single number.  However, I can say that if you find yourself missing a lot, you can focus on weapons/attacks that may do less damage (or cost more stamina, or have less chance to do a special effect, or hits more slowly, or...) but will hit much more often.  In fact, for some fights, you may well shift tactics because you want to hit frequently for low damage (for instance, to disrupt spellcasters), while at other times, you may want to hit infrequently for high damage - for instance, against monsters with high armor absorbtion, or against creatures which do damage back to their attackers on each blow.  Short form - if you want to play a character specializing in hitting fast and often for distracting-style attacks, you can do so.  If you want to specialize in disabling monsters so your rogues can run in for a backstab, you can do so.  If you want to play a generalist with some capability in a variety of attacks so you can change per fight/party, you can do so (with, of course, the caveat that you won't be quite as efficient as a specialist in the role, in general.)<br><br>I think everyone will be pleased with the variety of different combat specialties you can create, and for a given character, the range of attacks you can choose from on a per-hit basis.  We've done a lot to make combat non-repetitive, but at the same time accomodate new folks.  And, of course, to balance it as well.<br><br>-jg<p><b>Concerning NPCs</b><br>Talking to NPCs will play a big part in understanding the rich background and history of the world.  The team has done an incredible job on making it so that the things that NPCs say can be easily changed to suit the needs of the game.  Good portions of the NPCs dialog is written by our own fiction writers.<br><br>Our NPCs will definatly have some depth to them.<br><br><br>"G." Bob<br>Community Coordinator<br>"Nemo enim fere saltat sobrius, nisi forte insanit."<p><b>Dodging Fireballs and Why We Can't</b><br>In the case of Origin, you can't dodge a fireball primarily because our game is based around stats, strategies and tactics more than it is on reflexes.  This isn't an accident, really - RPGs are a different kind of game, and we are an RPG.  <br><br>Some companies are entering the Massively Multiplayer 3D shooter fray.  Tanarus and 10-Six are already there, and NeoCron, PlanetSide and Dark Sector are in production.  Expect all of these to be a lot more action-based.  <br><br>But so far, they really haven't caught on, largely because the 'feel' of them isn't as good as playing smaller games, like Quake 2 or Unreal: Tournament.  Engines can only handle so much, but that's only part of the story.  Network bandwidth really starts to crimp your style  The more people in an area, the laggier it gets, especially in an actionbased game (this is less a problem in games like UO and EQ where sitting and chatting is a large part of the routine).  To top it off, if you have persistent characters, you better be danged sure the game is fair, which means your servers have to do a lot of validation.<br><br>At this current time, there are only two choices, one is to reduce the quality of the gameplay experience to a point where it is far below smaller FPSs, in hopes that the sheer cool factor will offset the lower performance and gameplay quality.  The other is to have a series of 'arenas', with finite limits to how many people can enter them (sorta like EQ zones with limits).<br><br>How will the future of technology affect this?  Hard to say, really.  I think the machines at home will be ready to run the games in, say, two/three years, and the advancing speed of technology combined with an aggressive LOD/PM system could really hurry this along.  The graphics will still be lower in quality than their smaller counterparts, but then they will probably still be as good as, say, U:T now.<br><br>The real holdup though isn't the engines but the network connections.  It takes a lot of pipe to handle the amount of network traffic a MMPFPS would generate in flashpoint locations, and I don't think a 56K modem will cut it.  Cable modems and DSL could greatly reduce that, but so far, the penetration of broadband into american homes is still far lower than any internet game developer would like it to be.<br><br>On the other hand, it's worth noting that I haven't really sat down to think about all of the issues here.  The Dark Sector/Neocron/Planetside teams may have found ways to solve all of these issues.  Which would be great for me, cuz I love FPSes. =)<br><br>Ubiq<p><b>Concerning the Interface</b><br>The UI elements are rendered in 3D, but are actually 2D elements. Our artists can generally make the small detail elements look better by hand-drawing them than relying on all the various hardware devices to do a good job down at the pixel level.<br><br>WRT clunky interfaces: I hear you. I've recently been playing a certain MMRPG and I find its mouse camera interface to be completely unusable. I come from a 3D FPS background, so I'm pretty sensitive to how important a good control interface is; basically it needs to be as intuitive and unobtrusive as possible. I recall many weeks tweaking the various parameters for an FPS control interface, trying to get it "just right".<br><br>I think the recent announced of UO3D is proof that client graphics really are separate from the game mechanics.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>More On User Interface</b><br>There will be no 2d sprites used for the interface.  All of the UI will be rendered in 3d.  Also the UI will not be stationary.  The gumps (your backpack, inventory window, etc.) are movable, resizable, and able to be toggled on and off as you see fit.  Also most of the gumps have a background on them which allows you to see the 3d world sitting behind it.<br><br>  Origin view will be via a moveable camera.  Where you place that camera is up.  You will be able to zoom in and out and rotate the camera around you.  A lot of our design decisions would make it pretty hard to do a first person view.<br><br>The LOD system helps quite a bit with allowing us to update graphics.  I believe Hyperion has made a few good posts about this.<br><br>Medwynd<br>User Interface Programmer<br>UWO: Origin<p><b>Concerning The Clipping Plane</b><br>Clipping plane will be the last thing we change for frame rate, since there is a gameplay advantage to it (i.e, I could sit outside of your viewing distance, shooting you in the head with arrows while you wonder what's going on).  Exactly how we scale down graphics for lower cards is tbd, but we'll probably err on the side of making things ugly (removing polies, lowering texture res, removing hueing, removing cool effects, etc.) before bringing in the viewing distance.<br><br>-jg<p><b>Dual Monitor Support?</b><br>It works now (many of our worldbuilders use it) but it's low on the list to support for ship, so no promises.<br><br>If it's easy to maintain, and we support windowed mode through ship (we might get a speed increase by dropping it) we'll see. :)<br><br>-jg
<!-- /Indent --></font></td></tr></table><!-- End /Indent -->
<!-- End Story -->
<!-- Thanks -->
