			<font face="Arial" size="-1" color="Black">
<!-- From -->
Gaffer added some more to <a class=post href=http://vnboards.ign.com/message.asp?topic=6978600>this thread</a> on our <a class=post href=http://vnboards.ign.com/board.asp?brd=5166>DevBoard</a> so get in there and give them some feedback on the combat system!:
<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
You can try to stun someone, then quickly switch weapons and try to get in a bladed attack, but I'd recommend instead:
 <br> 
 <br> a) Have a guy in strong armor with either a blunt or sharp weapon in front, doing attacks with high taunt factors, while guys with high delay bashing weapons stand near doing interuptable attacks with high knockdown factors since most attacks are after the primary tank, while rogues are waiting to run in and backstab once they get a stun in but otherwise dart in and out of combat so that they don't get hit in their light armor.  Meanwhile, archers and mages plink from the sides trying not to annoy the monster enough to hit them.
 <br> b) several people attack the monster with moves that are low damage but increase their defense, while a monk does high KD chance flying kicks into the combat (which moves him in from a range, and then bounces him out of combat, as seen in some of the movie snaps).  When the monster gets stunned/knocked down, the defenders briefly switches to attacks that are strong against vulnerable opponents.
 <br> 
 <br> That'd be a few of the more obvious ways to coordinate melee groups.  Of course, when you mix in different types of mages, archers, tech weapons, and multiple monster opponents, it gets even more interesting.  Never mind, of course, the opportunities with group PvP.  High offense while getting lots of healing cast on you is of course what I'd expect lots of inexperienced groups to do, and this should be a valid tactic too, at least for some fights.
 <br> 
 <br> Oh, if you want to solo, you could dual wield a mace and dagger (or other combo) but you'll need to spend a bunch of anhks to get the specialized attacks with both kinds of weapons, and your chances won't be as good as if you had someone else helping you...
 <br> 
 <br>Oh, and for those of you worried that the tactics I gave will be repeated in every fight (and thus get tedious over time)
 <br> 
 <br> - monster groups are mixed, and fighting a group that's half casters (or rogues, or stunners, or...) will be quite different than fighting single monsters or all normal melee
 <br> - different monsters are annoyed by different things; the next group you meet might really hate people doing interruptable attacks and go out of their way to disrupt them even if annoyed at someone else, or seek out spellcasters specifically, or really hate particular kinds of moves.  It's all part of the AI's (say it with me) data driven, table driven, ultra expandible architecture.
 <br> - some monsters have different protections; a monster that reflects damage to you on every attack isn't going to respond as well to your lots-of-low-damage-attacks strategy that worked so well against the spellcaster last fight 
 <br> 
 <br> You've got to think on your feet even in PvE fights :)
 <br> 
 <br> -jg
