			<font face="Arial" size="-1" color="Black">
<!-- From -->
From Gaffer on our <a class=post href=http://vnboards.ign.com/board.asp?brd=5166>DevBoard</a>:
<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
There's still lots of tweaking, but here's some of the ways we're balancing things in melee combat as it stands:
 <br> 
 <br> Heavy weapons tend to stun or knock oppenents down, and do more damage through armor.  They also tend to attack slowly, can be interrupted, and cost more stamina to use.
 <br> Light weapons tend to attack more quickly, for less damage.  They rarely stun or KD people.
 <br> Blunt weapons raise the chances for stun/KD, but do lower damage against many (non-chain) armor types.
 <br> Sharp weapons tend to do good damage (especially against vulnerable (stunned, unarmored, or knocked down) opponents), but have little chance for stun/KD themselves.  They do tend to cause some bleeding damage (which basically is damage over time after the attack).
 <br> 
 <br> More specifically, one handed swords tend to do good damage in less time than almost anything else, but cause the least special effects (stun, KD, interruption, etc.)  Heavy swords are more varied, but can be interupted during attacks, are slower, etc.  Axes are slow, cause all 3 of stun/knockdown and bleeding in small amounts, but take more stamina to use.   Heavy hammers have great chances to disable opponents, but at high cost in stamina usage and interuptability.  Obviously, there are many more kinds of weapons, including tech weapons, but the short form is that all of them have strengths and weaknessess.
 <br> 
 <br> Attack styles have their own balance - there are many spectactular ranged attacks, but they come at the cost of wearing less armor, draining more stamina, and being interruptable.  And there is lots more variation, including at the upper ranges attacks which cause spell-like effects in addition to damage (vampiric attacks, attacks which can lower statistics, fatiguing attacks, etc.)  Some attack styles are general - they can be used with any 1 handed weapon, etc.  Some are specialized per weapon - 1h sword attacks, etc (in fact, these attacks are the ones that tend to let you use your weapon's tendencies to the fullest)
 <br> 
 <br> This may seem like a lot of variation and like it might be complex to use (and to min/max, it is, by design - there are even more variables involved than mentioned.) - but the short form is, play the kind of character you want to play, and you'll find things work out reasonably naturally - if you play a healer type using blunt weapons, your attacks will be much more likely to disable your opponent than to cause actual damage.  Your knife-wielding monk bretheren can use this opportunity to slip in and do serious damage.  Etc.  And, for newbies, you're presented with very few choices initially, but each choice can open up a host of future possibilities, so by the time you're high level, you've made a lot of choices - but only one at a time.  And, if you don't like the way things went, you can always back down the skill tree and try something else.
 <br> 
 <br> This is, as always, subject to change before ship in many ways...we're a'tweakin.
 <br> 
 <br> -jg
