			<font face="Arial" size="-1" color="Black">
<!-- From -->
From Gaffer on the <a class=post href=http://boards.xrgaming.net/forums/list.php?f=96>CoNB DevBoard</a>:
<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
<i>That's good to hear :)  A much smaller question in the meantime:
<BR>
<BR>Do all the modifiers due to item type, enchantments, skills, and so on, boil down into just a few statistics (as EQ seemed to be) that are used to work out to-hit and damager?
<BR></i>
<BR>
<BR>Yes, you end up with a small number of total numbers (after all, in the end, you're interested in two things:  Did I hit, and how much damage did I do?  In the interim, there are a lot of numbers (+chance to hit, +damage, damage roll, stamina cost, +chance to defense, +damage absorbtion, and more) used to calculate the final answer, and a ton of ways to modify the above on a case by case basis.
<BR>
<BR><quote><i>
<BR>Or, alternatively, are all these modifiers applied one after another 'on the fly'?
<BR>It seems to me that the latter would be more math (and hence, processor) intensive, so I assume its the former...
<BR></i></quote>
<BR>
<BR>It's almost impossible to do these modifiers "on the fly" because, implemented naively, it is too expensive to calculate, and also - pretend you could fight directly across server boundaries.  You'd need to have some way to get all of these modifiers from the attacker to the defender because you couldn't know ahead of time any of the information about the defender as you resolve your attack.  However, despite being almost impossible, it's doable.  In fact, we did it.  There are some <i>very</i> clever tricks we've done (especially <b>Gunner10</b>) to make this work both efficiently and across server boundaries.  Er, as an end user, it may not be obvious just how cool this is, but in the future, there are very few limitations on the kinds of cool items we can add, or modify on the fly.  For instance, if we put the names of the top 10 cat jugglers into a list, we could add to our treasure tables swords enchanted to specifically modify damage against one of those persons randomly chosen if we wanted to - very cool dynamic enchantments like that.  Not for free, of course (keeping lists takes new code, and has other issues) but this particular system could even let that sort of thing happen efficiently.  Again, the goal is not so much to have lots of kinds of catjuggler-slaying swords in the world at ship, but to give the Live group free rein with that kind of thing forever into the future for events, new systems, and what have you.  It's very open.
<BR>
<BR>Er, I don't think I've done a good job of explaining why it's so cool (any example I could give would sound like a promise of something we're not talking about yet) but the future should bear it out when we have neat stuff that no one else can do ;)
<BR>
<BR><quote><i>My original question was directed more at this -- if just a few attack and defense stats (calculated from all the modifiers) are used, how are they used once calculated?
<BR></i></quote>
<BR>
<BR>The above bits I gave are approximately right, but there's so much tweaking to do that the specifics probably aren't interesting yet, sadly.
<BR>
<BR><quote><i>And while I'm at it -- well done to the dev team for their approach to the fans in this pre-release time.  I hope the live team can live up to the example.</i></quote>
<BR>
<BR>The live team isn't fully formed yet, but you've probably spoken to a good bit of it already - lots of folks working on the game just want to keep making it better and better (and heck, it takes so much effort to make these engines that you get to work on lots of the really cool stuff once you've got one to work with!).
<BR>
<BR>Glad to hear we're doing a good job though :)
<BR>
<BR>-jg
<!-- /Indent --></font></td></tr></table><!-- End /Indent -->
<!-- End Story -->
<!-- Thanks -->
Thanks!
<!-- End Thanks -->
</font>


