			<font face="Arial" size="-1" color="Black">
<!-- From -->
From the <a class=post href=http://boards.uo2.com/>Official Boards</a>:
<!-- End From -->
<!-- Begin Story -->
<!-- Indent --><table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" border="0"><tr><td width="15"><img src="/images/blank.gif" width=15 height=1 border=0 alt=""></td><td><font face="Arial" size="-1" color="#000068"><!-- End Indent-->
<b>The Techno-Prophet</b><br>Blackthorn does indeed become the Techno-Prophet, but as we've seen from the Prequel Fiction, Lord Blackthorn is very much his own person.  The Prime Overlord created the current incarnation of the Techno-Prophet, but it quickly found out that it didn't control him.<br><br>Keep in mind that the Janissars who served Blackthorn were not the original ones who served the Prime Overlord 200 years ago.  These Loyalists swore allegiance to only one figure - the Techno-Prophet.<br><br>And yes, Blackthorn and the Prime Overlord fought for control of Logos.  Now isn't that a fight you would have wanted to see? ;)<br><br>Ahriman<p><b>Interface Art</b><br>Actually most of the interface art is pretty much placeholder art.  While there will be the toolbelt and the button tray what components those are made up from are still in the works.  You are correct that the slots in the toolbelt are accessed via the function keys.  The horizontal bars are for health and xp.  As for the button tray those are just icons that we pulled to fill it up.  Some have a function that doesnt even correspond to the icon and some spots have a icon that does nothing.  But there are inventory, paperdoll, and chat box icons in there that are functioning.<br><br>Medwynd<br>User Interface Programmer<br>UWO: Origin<p><b>Promises</b><br>Xeno, you raised a number of very good points.  Allow me to raise some counters to those arguments.<br><br>First, as much as most of us can read a post from a development member saying "that would be a cool idea", there is a large segment of the population who walk away from the conversation feeling like they had been promised the feature.  In UO we can see this happening a number of times.  (Player Races, Alchemy, Necromancy, Townstones, etc.)  Sometimes we have to drop work on a needed feature to work on a system that the player base decided was "promised" to them.  This really hurts the development process in the long run, and leads to a feeling amongst the player base that they're not being listened to.<br><br>I don't think that the people who believe we made a promise are overly emotional either.  Sometimes it's a case of honest misunderstanding.  Much like the game "post office", messages often change as they move from one source to another.  Equally important is that the people who suggest some of these ideas feel a level of vested interest in seeing them implemented. If a player sends a 10 page document on "flaming kitten juggling" and the next week Ubiq writes "we're probably going to put in the flaming kitten juggling" then it's perfectly natural that the person who sent him the document will walk away angry if, for one reason or another, flaming kitten juggling doesn't make it into the game.<br><br>Look at what happened when we removed from our FAQ the segments on housing and rideable mounts.  There were a number of people who felt very cheated by the change in our plans....and I can't fault them for that.  We messed up by putting information up that we couldn't guarantee development on.<br><br>You are, however, quite correct when you say that this makes getting player feedback much more difficult.  It's part of the trade off.  Let's look at a recent example in Ultima Online.  We want to put in rewards for veteran players.  We develop it to the point where we KNOW we can implement it with no problem at all, and we release the suggestion to the player base.  The player base decides that they don't like the rewards offered, and we have to go back to the drawing board.  Because we no longer announce anything without knowing we can actually do it we had to invest a much larger number of man hours into Veteran rewards then we would have to if we promised first, delivered later.  As I said, it's a trade off.  I think that the cost is worth it though.  The players are once more trusting us when we say we can deliver something.<br><br>So given that it's more difficult to get player feedback, how do we get around it?  Well, for one thing we put alot more people on the job.  We spend more time reading message boards, and making reports based upon what you, the players, want out of the game.  In Origin, we have both Cynthe and myself reading hundreds upon hundreds of messages each day.  We hold events like the World Faire where we talk with the fans.  We pay attention to the e-mails you send us.  <br><br>It's more work, but in return nobody feels like they were lied to.  That alone is worth it.<br><br><br>"G." Bob<br>Community Coordinator<br>"Nemo enim fere saltat sobrius, nisi forte insanit."<p><b>Getting Around</b><br>Because moongates are the fastest way to travel we have 'layover' stops to smaller towns. This way you don't to into Yew in order to get all the way back to Britain, just take the express in Chantry...<br>The only time you should have to use some shoe leather is to get to really dangerous places. That's so Newbies don't pop out of the moongate and say, "Hey, that's a cute little slasher over there, I'll go tame it..."<p><b>Continent to Continent Travel</b><br><i>ok... what if pk'ers (not just pkers but u know what im getting at) hung out on the border between the PvP zone and the standard zone.... could they have a go at everyone that comes into the PvP zone and if they are out matched then just run off into the non-PvP zone - thus they can't be killed?????</i><br><br>Not a problem - the only 'borders' between the continents will be the moongates - there won't be any dividing lines between wild lands and safe lands, like you might see at the edge of a UO town.  It's more like the Trammel/Felucca division.<br><br>Those flagged as criminals will be stopped from entering a moongate that goes to the safe lands at the code level - the moongate simply won't function if you try to go through it.  We're doing this because, if you were to find a way to get around the masses of guards at each moongate who will attack you on sight, when you got to the safe lands, there would be no way for people to mete out justice upon thy PK-ish head and whatnot.  It would be unfair to some if they could not fight the criminals in the safe lands, hence criminals can't go there.<br><br>Cheers,<br><br>Windfeather<br>Designer o' Stuff<br><br><i>Real life is the ultimate roleplaying game.</i><p><b>Realistic Spawn</b><br>The problem with 'realistic' spawn is that monsters that spawn good stuff (i.e. orcs have armor!) end up getting over-hunted, whereas monsters that spawn nothing (i.e. bats can't carry gold!) end up getting ignored.  And if a monster ends up getting ignored, then it sure was a waste of a monster model.  Players are very quick at finding the shortest way to the cheese, and you can only give enough exp to replace the gold by wrecking your balanced curve.<br><br>That's not to say that we haven't tried to make treasure appropriate, and you're right in the fact that the Adventurer's Economy is our primary focus - we don't want monster loot to make craftsmen obsolete, we want it to instead make them move valuable.  But the emphasis is still on being sure the game is fun and has a strong community much more than being 'realistic'.<br><br>Ubiq<p><b>More On Loot</b><br>As an addition, I don't see a conflict between realistic loot and fun.  I think both are eminently possible in the same system.  If something is not a sentient creature (say, a fire kitten), it doesn't have to carry gold to have value; its body parts could be important reagents for making other things, or have some sort of intrinsic value that lets them be sold, which turns them into a gold equivalent.<br><br>Windfeather<br>Designer o' Stuff<br><br><i>Real life is the ultimate roleplaying game.</i><p><b>Seamless World</b><br>Exactly right. Our map is 'seamless' the way UO's are (there are server boundaries that you can see across). There are exceptions to that - Dungeons, for example, are not connected seamlessly, largely because trying to put them in the same world would slow down things for both people above and below ground.<br><br>Ubiq<p><b>Reputation Stuff</b><br><i>Will we be capable of knowing how many points are in our PvP buffer? How easy will it be to discern if we kill, loot or steal from someone that we will lose a significant amount of reputation/PvP points?</i><br><br>Hmm...the short answer is 'No.'  Here's why:<br><br>Real-life studies have repeatedly shown that the odds of getting caught, rather than the severity of the penalty, prevent people from breaking the law.<br><br>As it is designed right now, unless you decide to throw caution to the wind and play an outlaw, the system relies on you to have good judgement.  Part of that judgement is a healthy concern that you will get 'caught' by the Reputation System if you act in a violent manner too frequently.<br><br>For the typical player who wants a PvE environment, but with the ability to fight others when necessary (or consensual), your buffer should rarely run out.  It regenerates over time - unless you have a hot temper and attack, loot or steal from every single person who annoys you in the slightest, you shouldn't ever use it up.<br>  <br>For the system to work properly, it needs to instill a sense of caution - a fear of being caught - in the typical player.  If I were to tell you exactly how much someone costs to kill, and exactly what your point bank is, it would actually increase the amount of antisocial play because the element of caution would be removed: You would know exactly what you could get away with (i.e., not be caught committing a crime by depleting your buffer) at any one time.<br><br>However, I want to be generous enough in how many buffer points you have that I also install a healthy fear of being killed for acting like a jerk (i.e., getting 'caught' for being a jerk) in the mind of the hypothetical grief player.  I can think of many, many instances over my online career in which people would not have behaved as they did if they knew a sword could come swinging at their neck.<br><br>We'll be watching and listening closely in beta to see how things go.  My goal = a polite, armed society that can police itself.<br><br>Windfeather<br>Designer o' Stuff<br><br><i>Real life is the ultimate roleplaying game.</i><p><b>More Reputation Stuff</b><br><i>What made you decide to have both 'naughtly points' AND a 'Reputation' value? From my thinking, these would always move in parallel, thus why have both?? Is the rate-of-increase different between the two? Could you give an in-game example of why you decided to have BOTH these factors?</i><br><br>Sure.  Reputation is a point counter that reflects what you have done to other people.  That part is easy enough to understand.  'Naughty Points' I probably should have called something else, but I was trying to be cute at the time.  In my design docs, 'Naughty Points' are actually called the 'PvP Frequency Modifier'.  They track how <b>often</b> you are engaged in PvP, whether from an attacker/looter/stealer standpoint, and also, to a lesser extent, when you are attacked by someone.<br><br>If I did not have the PvP Modifier, and instead I based your reputation hit on the other person's reputation, we would have a 'Blue PK' sort of problem: Someone might never kill, loot, or steal, yet be a blocker, kill stealer, or whatever and be expensive to kill because they had a good reputation.<br><br>Since I can't track everything someone could do to be a pest (how do you track people insulting your friends, for example?), I chose to let the players do it for me by voting with their swords: if you get killed all the time, you will have a very high PvP Frequency value.  The game will think, 'this guy sure does get PKd a lot more than most people.  Maybe he deserves it.' and make you a cheap kill.<br><br>Moral: If someone is being a pest in Origin, lop off their head, because they are probably a pest to everyone and a cheap kill, even if they appear to have a good reputation.  People will always rise or sink to their own level.<br><br>Hope that helps :)<br><br>Windfeather<br>Designer o' Stuff<br><br><i>Real life is the ultimate roleplaying game.</i><p><b>Clothing</b><br>There is a tremendous amount of different clothing, which works in layers - so you can wear an overcoat over your pneumatic armor, or whatever.  Check out the recent screenshots to see some of this.<br><br>It's hueable too, of course.  Lotsa options.<br><br>-jg<p><b>Concerning the Hopefully Soon-to-be Released Video</b><br>As far as I know, it will be the one from the Faire, but it is possible that the dev team will want to add/change something.  That decision would be up to them, of course, not me, so I won't know until it's time to release it, most likely.  <br><br>When we do release it, though, we'll be sure to make it clear as to whether or not it's been altered in any way.<br><br>_____________________________________<br><b>Cynthe</b><br>Community Coordinator<br>UWO: ORIGIN<p><b>Playing In A Window</b><br>We currently are playable from inside a window (actually, the video was shot in windowed mode.)  However - it's still up in the air whether or not we'll allow this at ship, for a few reasons:<br><br>a) Games which haven't allowed alt-tabbing have had LOTS fewer hack/macro program attempts (which means a more even playing field, lowered support costs for us (which you pay for), etc.)<br>b) 3D games traditionally run much faster in fullscreen mode (the operating system is less involved) and so we should probably design for that instead of adding code and dev time to always support running in windowed mode.<br><br>However, this is still up in the air.  After all, a LOT of people like to have the freedom to use ICQ/etc without jumping through a lot of hoops.  We totally get that.  It's also nice to be able to bring up a website to get info on the game while you're playing.  It's nice to run two clients at once if your hardware allows (though that way lies abuse potential, I admit) Some of our devs run that way now to test stuff, so it is possible, to all those of you saying it'd be unplayable :)<br><br>So...it's an open issue.  I'm interested in opinions, though we may have to be Tyrants on this issue in the final analysis.<br><br>-jg<p><b>Level of Detail</b><br>To be specific, we don't use progressive mesh technology, we use Level of Detail technology.  In progressive meshes, vertices (and therefore polys) are removed at runtime, whereas with LODs, you actually have 3-5 versions of every piece of art with different levels of quality.<br><br>PMs are a very, very cool technology, but in our experimentation with them, we decided that they aren't quite ready for prime-time.  PMs can often make bad choices when choosing which vertex to remove, which can result in some funky wierdness.  Our artists don't like giving up that control, and fixing the problem requires either art time or CPU cycles.<br><br>The other problem is that PMs get messy when combined with some of our other technologies, most notably huable, deforming meshes rebuilt and retextured on the fly.<br><br>LODs pretty much do the same thing, only they are more reliable from the artists perspective and less CPU intensive overall.  Downsides are that it takes more art time to do 'em, and people who are watching carefully can see 'popping' from one LOD level to another.  Fortunately, our artists kick ass, and have managed to get our LODs to the point where the guy who wrote the LOD system, Hyperion, pretty much can't tell when they switch.<br><br>(Forgive any errors, I'm no programmer, just a designer with time to kill)<br><br>Ubiq<p><b>32 bit versus 16 bit</b><br>32 bit color will look a lot better than 16 bit... erm... 65536 times better. We use a lot of subtle techniques that don't hold up well in 16 bit - you can see the results (banding) in some of the screenshots Cynthe posted that were created on her Voodoo 3.<br><br>Most modern cards will perform just as well in 32 bit mode. 24 bit mode is very poorly supported in hardware - we won't be using it. It doesn't make good sense from a pixel stride point of view, anyway; hardware likes pixels aligned at nice even powers of 2.<br><br>Interface elements do not scale; they wouldn't look good if they did.<br><br><b><i>Hyperion</i></b><p><b>Interface Philosophy</b><br>Re: interface scaling - Our interface was not designed to 'frame' the screen and look pretty, like most games. Instead, it was designed to be as customizable as possible, much like UO, where you can keep the gumps open you want, and place the gumps you like wherever on your screen you like 'em. Thus, our gumps were designed to float, not to be fixed, and handle multiple resolutions/window sizes much better.<br><br>Ubiq
<!-- /Indent --></font></td></tr></table><!-- End /Indent -->
<!-- End Story -->
<!-- Thanks -->
Thanks!
<!-- End Thanks -->
</font>


